course: dictionary
lesson: 9916
title: Tamil: a language, a people, a word for things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Tamil is a word with three jobs. It can name a language: «She can speak Tamil.» It can name a person: «My friend is a Tamil.» It can describe something: «We ate Tamil food at the festival.» It always starts with a capital letter.
explain: Use the context to work out which job it does. If a verb like speak or learn comes before it, it is probably the language. If the word after it is a thing, like food or script, Tamil is describing it. If it has an s at the end, as in Tamils, it means more than one person.
explain: Context works across the world too. «In Singapore English (Singlish), someone says, "Can lah!"» Singlish mixes English with Malay, Chinese languages and Tamil. So a Tamil word can be part of everyday talk far away from India and Sri Lanka.
fact: Tamil is a language spoken by more than 70 million people.
fact: Most Tamil speakers live in the state of Tamil Nadu in south India and in Sri Lanka.
fact: Tamil is also spoken in Singapore and Malaysia, and by communities in the UK and Canada.
fact: Tamil is one of the oldest languages still spoken, with poems over 2,000 years old.
fact: A Tamil is a person whose people and language are Tamil, and «Tamils» means more than one.
exercise: «Tamil script has rounded letters.» In this sentence, what does Tamil describe? | A festival | A person | *The script | A town
exercise: «My neighbour is a Tamil, and her family moved from Sri Lanka.» What is a Tamil? | *A person whose people and language are Tamil | A street in London | A kind of curry | A bird
exercise: Where is Tamil mainly spoken? | *Tamil Nadu in south India | Australia | Scotland
test: «She speaks Tamil fluently.» What does Tamil name here? | *A language | A festival | A place | A river
test: «Tamil food is very spicy.» What does Tamil describe? | A language | A person | A mountain | *Food
exam: Which sentence uses Tamil to name a people? | *«My neighbour is a Tamil.» | «The Tamil script is round.» | «She speaks Tamil.» | «I like Tamil food.»
